Z||000000||GTFiero1||11-03-2001||11:44 PM||||never ever buy a kent brand bike. i have a Kent CONCORD PRO FEVER dual suspention moutain bike an let me say that it is the biggest peice ofn crap ever. iva had it for a yera and already its life is gone. they are low quality. first it started when my rims bent. i dont even do anything. they're extreamly weak. my friend had a Pacific dual suspention bike and he does more on it than i do and his rims arent bent. second, the chain gard just shattered off. i was pedaling and it just shattered and a screw from it got into my chain and locked the hole bike up from pedaling but i took the screw out and its fine BUT i cannot shift the left handle gear into 3rd or else the chain falls off. there are so many creaks and squeaks on the bike. i went around oiling everything there is yet there still there. there is a bunch of little problems but the big hit happened tonight. for no reason, the crank arm just busted off. it will not stay back on. s friend who has a fiero said that he had a friend that had the same bike and the same thing happened. low quality KENT<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroMontreal||11-04-2001||12:23 AM||||I know how you feel man.. I had a Minelli(sp?) Downhill bike with Double Crown Suspention in the front and rear susp. I did a jump once and my front susp snapped, I ended up flying over the bike.. didn't get injured though.. but lot's of my friends got a good laugh.. I brought the bike back.. the put a brand new pair of front susp. in the front to replace the broken one.. 1 day later.. same thing happens. I go back to the store, I'm really pissed, the reps from Minelli have a meeting and decide to do a recall on the bike.. I get my money back and end up buying a significly more expensive bike..<P>I now have a Giant Warp DS-1.. yay <P>I think this all happened in '99<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigfieromontreal_1.gif"> <BR>1986 Fiero GT, STOCK Wingless, <BR>-Alex President of FieroMontreal... <A HREF="http://www.fieromontreal.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieromontreal.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||rodmcneill||11-04-2001||12:35 AM||||As the owner of a bicycle shop I have to use the old adage "you get what you pay for". We see discount store bikes all the time that have not been properly assembled. These bikes use really low grade parts and for the most part, only look like mountain bikes. They really are not designed to go off road and be ridden hard. Bikes come in different frame sizes, smaller frames for smaller riders, larger frames for taller riders. You have no choice in frame size or any other component when you buy a bike at "Wally World". Do yourself a favor, if you decide to stay in cycling visit your local bike shop,talk to the experts who will professionally assemble and fit the bike to you. Most bike shops give at least 1 free tuneup with a new bike purchase. If money is a little tight consider a quality used bike that has been refurbished. A quality used bike will hold up way better than any Kent brand. I don't know about other shops but we let customers take bikes for test rides. You will be surprised at how well a good bike shifts,brakes, is more comfortable than a discount store bike. Any questions, just ask.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroMontreal||11-04-2001||12:38 AM||||Just as a side note, My bike was bought at a bike store and had all the different options like frame size etc..<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigfieromontreal_1.gif"> <BR>1986 Fiero GT, STOCK Wingless, <BR>-Alex President of FieroMontreal... <A HREF="http://www.fieromontreal.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fieromontreal.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||GTFiero1||11-04-2001||12:51 AM||||i got this one at a Target for 200 bucks. this was after my stunt bike got stolen so i didnt really have the money for a really good one. it looked nice though, nice blue and yellow combo. It came with a dent in the swing arm for no extra charge. im considering getting an aluminum dual suspension bike now. its at target also. there arent any bike shops near me. there is one but all they have are over priced stunt bikes. 500 buks for a 20in bike tht has one brake, no kick stand and a plastic seat. i know they produce these things for like 20 bucks too. go back 10 years ago and they would only be 100bucks.<P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
Z||000005||sloth85GT||11-04-2001||01:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroMontreal:<BR><B>I now have a Giant Warp DS-1.. yay </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>YOU TOO??? SWEET! I was just gonna say, if you want a good bike, go with the Giant Warp DS-1. I've got the 2000 model red one. But I have a silver Psylo XC upgrade and a few other trinkets like carbon colour fenders, Wellgo clipless pedals, and a Selle Italia yellow seat. Sweet ass ride.<P>And about the Kent bike....don't know what to say, never heard of such a bike, probably a local no-name brand. And to you people thinking about Millenium brand Wal-Mart bikes, don't even think about them, worthless. Go to a bike shope and buy a bike with a reputable name like Giant, Trek, Specialized, Cannondale or Marin. Some bikes suck, if you ride hard, you have to pay a lot, you get what you pat for with bikes.<BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.mts.net/~kuzma/fieronl.gif">     <BR>Dan<BR>1985 Silver GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||LT1 Chop Top||11-04-2001||03:24 AM||||I actually have one one of those dual suspension Concord pacific (i think).  I have had it for three years, and besides having to have the gears re-adjusted, I have beat the dog poo out of it in the Colorado Rockies the last three summers, as well as the lame trails here.  It is still in good shape, no problems, but many MANY flats.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroFanaticus2||11-04-2001||09:52 AM||||I used to assemble bikes for chain stores (K-Mart, Shopko, Walmart, Sports Authority, Target).  After assembling more than 6000 bikes and doing endless repairs and customer bikes I figured out that most chain store bikes are crap.  BUT, you also get what you pay for.  Pacific bikes are a good bike, also a pain to assemble.  The $300 - $500 Mongoose bikes are good as well.  The Kent Bikes I had assembled for sports Authority were the biggest hunks of crap I've ever taken a wrench to.  Pedal wouldn't screw in half the time, bolts would strip under little pressure, chains would break.  They always had some sort of problem right out of the box.  I'd buy a Huffy from Shopko before I would even think about a Kent.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Neal||11-04-2001||12:26 PM||||OMG, im running away from this thread in FEAR.<P>The attack of the cheap garbage bikes.  NEVER NEVER buy a bike that isnt purchased at a professional bike shop, they know what there doing, "wall mart" has no hope in hell...<P>Oh yes, I do work at one of the top shops in my city, see junk bikes day in and day out, biggest reason people buy that crap is they have never taken the time to have a good talk with a real bike shop, SO many little things will make you never consider a "wall mart" bike again.<P>And for anyone who cares at employee pricing I paid over 3000 for my bike, and if you ride lots it IS worth the money, but even a 300 dollar bike shop bike is thousand times better than a "wall mart" bike.||||reg||1
