Z||000010||ray b||11-04-2001||05:02 PM||||learn the grades of parts that are used china is mostly crapp copys of better jap parts but even good jap co. make cheap low lines of parts.<BR>shimono sp? is best but they make like 10 diffrent grades of parts. 600 was top end but they change the names on newer stuff all the time.<BR>roadrace bikes campi [italy]is king but very hi priced i have had some used cheap but very hard to find.<BR>usa parts are best for m/t bikes but very hi-end.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000011||Standard||11-04-2001||06:05 PM||||The debate between Campy and Shimano will never end.  I love the Campy Record stuff, but it's a bit too expensive and I don't like the ergopower levers.  I'd rather have Dura-Ace.  Then again, I'm not a road biker  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>As for department store bikes.. like others have said, you get what you pay for.  At the shop I work at you would get one free tune up and 2 years free adjustments, plus we'll fit you to the bike and install any accesories you get when you buy it.  <P>I spent over 3 grand on my bike, too.  At cost.  heh.  It's more than worth it.<P>As long as there's some bike guys in here.. I have 2 new forks for sale.  Both 1 1/8", steerer hasn't been cut.<P>'98 Manitou Spyder R.. Fork only, orange, alloy steerer.  Spring/elastomer and air dampened.  50 bucks + shipping<P>'01 Judy XC.. New in box, black, steel steerer.  All-travel (63, 80, or 100mm), external rebound adjustment.  100 bucks + shipping.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000012||JohnnyK||11-04-2001||06:28 PM||||Man, I used to race xc a long time ago (haven't actually ridden my bike in a few years). I loved it. Bought the parts all seperately and threw em together. It was a Maxam frame (made by an older bike shop that used to be around here), titanium.. XTR, Quadra21R's (They were new that year!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000013||WampusCat||11-04-2001||07:24 PM|||| <A HREF="http://216.247.213.144/specials/mba_santacruz.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://216.247.213.144/specials/mba_santacruz.htm</A> <P>or <A HREF="http://www.supergo.com" TARGET=_blank>www.supergo.com</A>   <P>If you have enough mechanical aptitude to keep a fiero running, you should be able to wrench on you own bike.  check out you local library for books on bicycle maint.<p>[This message has been edited by WampusCat (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||GTFiero1||11-04-2001||09:24 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by WampusCat:<P>If you have enough mechanical aptitude to keep a fiero running, you should be able to wrench on you own bike.  check out you local library for books on bicycle maint.<P>keep a fiero running?<BR>im guessing fieros are peices of crap? man, if some one new were to come here and serch around thinking about buying a fiero you guys would make them think otherwise. i see this all the time "hope you have some mechanical abilities" when someone says they wanna fiero. both engines in the fiero seem to hold up well, my autoshop teachers S-10 with a tech 4 has 170,000mi with no problems, original cap, rotor and wires too. my brothers firebird 2.8, 130,000+ miles  and he didnt do much to it but it never broke down.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Adam J. B.||||reg||1
